WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 458: Episode 458: Pope Leo I (The Great): The Sack of the Vandals & The Tome of Chalcedon (451–455)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.400
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our two-part saga on Pope Leo the Great.

4
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.160
[Rebecca] In our last episode, we witnessed Leo ride north to Halt Attila the Hun on the banks of the River Mincio.

5
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.680
[Rebecca] But history rarely allows great leaders a moment of rest.

6
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.200
[Rebecca] Just three years later, in 455 AD, an even deadlier storm struck Rome.

7
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.240
[Rebecca] The barbarian Vandal fleet sailed up the Tiber, seeking plunder and vengeance.

8
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:39.280
[Rebecca] With the Roman Emperor murdered by his own subjects, Pope Leo once again stood alone between a defenseless populace and total annihilation.

9
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:45.680
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary climax of Leo's life.

10
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:05.760
[George] In the mid-fifth century, the Eastern Roman Empire was being torn apart by violent theological controversies regarding the nature of Jesus Christ.

11
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:16.160
[George] In Constantinople and Alexandria, radical factions argued bitterly over whether Christ was purely divine, purely human, or a confused mixture of both.

12
00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:25.280
[George] The dispute was so fierce that bishops arrived at church councils accompanied by gangs of armed monks and gladiators, brawling in the aisles of cathedrals.

13
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:40.720
[George] Recognizing that the unity of Christendom was fracturing, Pope Leo sat at his desk in Rome and penned a masterpiece of theological clarity known to history as the Tome of Leo, his famous 28th epistle addressed to Bishop Flavian of Constantinople.

14
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.080
[George] With surgical Latin precision, Leo articulated the doctrine of the incarnation.

15
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:54.880
[George] Christ possessed two complete, unconfused and inseparable natures, truly God and truly man, united in one divine person.

16
00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:01.880
[George] In October 451, more than 500 bishops gathered across the Bosporus at the Council of Chalcedon.

17
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:10.360
[George] When Leo's papal legates stood before the assembly and read the tome aloud in solemn Latin and Greek, the council fathers were electrified.

18
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:18.120
[George] Moving with one voice, the bishops sprang to their feet and wept, chanting the historic declaration that echoed through the halls.

19
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.120
[George] This is the faith of the fathers.

20
00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.440
[George] This is the faith of the apostles.

21
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.240
[George] Peter has spoken through the mouth of Leo.

22
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:36.120
[George] In a single stroke, Leo had not only settled the greatest doctrinal crisis of the ancient church, but established the Bishop of Rome as the supreme theological arbiter of Christendom.

23
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:44.200
[George] Yet while Leo triumphed in the realm of the spirit, the secular Roman Empire was tearing itself to pieces in suicidal madness.

24
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:51.640
[George] In September 454, Western Roman Emperor Valentinian III committed an act of catastrophic folly.

25
00:02:51.640 --> 00:03:01.800
[George] Jealously suspicious of his brilliant general Flavius Aetius, the man who had defeated Attila the Hun, Valentinian summoned Aetius to his palace in Rome.

26
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:14.600
[George] As the aging general presented a financial report, Valentinian drew his imperial sword and, with the help of his eunuch chamberlain Heraclius, savagely hacked the defenseless general to pieces with his own hands.

27
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:26.120
[George] When a Roman diplomat asked the famous historian Sidonius Apollinurus what he thought of the assassination, the diplomat grimly remarked, The Emperor has cut off his right hand with his left.

28
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:29.160
[George] Six months later, retribution arrived.

29
00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:44.560
[George] In March 455, while Valentinian was inspecting archery practice on the campus Martius in Rome, two of Aetius's loyal barbarian bodyguards, Optila and Throstilla, drew their daggers and plunged them into the emperor's skull.

30
00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:49.520
[George] Valentinian fell dead in the dirt, the Theodosian dynasty was extinguished.

31
00:03:49.760 --> 00:04:04.880
[George] Into the power vacuum stepped Petronius Maximus, a wealthy, ruthless Roman senator who seized the throne, murdered the eunuch Chamberlain, and forced Valentinian's weeping widow, Empress Licinia Eudoxia, to marry him on pain of death.

32
00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:13.360
[George] Empress Eudoxia was the daughter of the Eastern Roman Emperor Theodosius I, a proud woman who despised the usurper Maximus.

33
00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:21.440
[George] Locked inside her royal apartments on the Palatine Hill, Eudoxia knew that her forced marriage was an outrage against honor and justice.

34
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:31.600
[George] Powerless to depose Maximus with Roman arms, Eudoxia turned her gaze across the Mediterranean Sea toward the ancient ruins of Carthage on the coast of North Africa.

35
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:41.440
[George] Carthage was now the capital of the Vandal kingdom, ruled by seventy-year-old Genseric, a lame, brilliant, and ruthless barbarian pirate king.

36
00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:55.840
[George] Genseric had conquered Roman North Africa, seized the imperial granaries, and built a colossal fleet of pirate warships that dominated the western Mediterranean, terrorizing merchant convoys and coastal cities from Sicily to Spain.

37
00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.120
[George] Furthermore, Genseric had a personal grievance.

38
00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:06.880
[George] His son Huneric had been betrothed to Eudoxia's daughter Eudocia, an agreement that the usurper Maximus had arrogantly broken.

39
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:16.480
[George] In secret, Eudoxia summoned a trusted servant, handed him a purse of gold and an encrypted royal letter, and dispatched him across the sea to Carthage.

40
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:24.480
[George] Her message was simple and devastating avenge the murder of Valentinian, overthrow the usurper Maximus, and seize Rome.

41
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.640
[George] Genseric did not hesitate.

42
00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:32.360
[George] The Vandal king had spent twenty years dreaming of plundering the fabulous wealth of the eternal city.

43
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:45.720
[George] Within weeks, the harbours of Carthage were alive with the sound of thousands of sailors hoisting sails, loading siege engines, and embarking an army of fifty thousand Vandal and Moorish warriors onto hundreds of warships.

44
00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:55.640
[George] In late May 455, lookout sails along the coast of Ostia spotted the crimson flags and dark hulls of the Vandal fleet darkening the horizon.

45
00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:00.120
[George] When the news reached Rome, blind panic paralyzed the population.

46
00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:08.520
[George] The Roman treasury was empty, there were no legions to man the massive Aurelian walls, there was no food stockpiled for a siege.

47
00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:14.840
[George] Emperor Petronius Maximus, who had spent months plotting for the crown, proved to be an utter coward.

48
00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:28.200
[George] Recognizing that the city was doomed, Maximus abandoned his throne, threw off his royal purple robes, disguised himself in the rags of a common slave, and attempted to gallop out the northern gate of Rome on a mule.

49
00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:34.760
[George] But as he rode past the Forum of Trajan, the furious Roman populace recognized their fleeing emperor.

50
00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:45.720
[George] Shouting that he had brought ruin upon the city, an enraged mob dragged Maximus from his mount, beat him with stones, stabbed him with daggers, and tore his body limb from limb.

51
00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:50.360
[George] His mutilated torso was dragged through the mud and hurled into the Tiber.

52
00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.840
[George] Rome was now completely leaderless.

53
00:06:52.840 --> 00:07:01.800
[George] No emperor, no senate, no army, and an armada of bloodthirsty barbarian pirates docking at the mouth of the river just sixteen miles away.

54
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:09.720
[George] On June 2, 455, the Vandal army marched up the Via Osciensis and appeared before the walls of Rome.

55
00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:17.200
[George] The city gates were barred, but the citizens were weeping on the parapets, knowing they could not withstand a single day of assault.

56
00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:23.760
[George] For the second time in three years, Pope Leo I stepped forward to become the solitary shield of his people.

57
00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:34.800
[George] Accompanied by his clergy carrying silver crosses and incense burners, the seventy-five-year-old Pope ordered the city gates thrown open at the porta portes on the right bank of the Tiber.

58
00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.880
[George] Flanked by his deacons, Leo walked directly into the advancing columns of barbarian horsemen.

59
00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:46.240
[George] Genseric, riding at the head of his armoured Vandal warriors, reigned in his horse.

60
00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:57.840
[George] The barbarian king was an Aryan Christian, a heretic in the eyes of Rome, yet he recognized the profound spiritual stature of the whitehaired bishop who had stood face to face with Attila the Hun.

61
00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:04.000
[George] As the two men met outside the city gates, Leo spoke with dignified, fearless authority.

62
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:14.000
[George] He acknowledged that Rome had no army to resist and offered no military opposition, but Leo demanded a sacred oath from the Vandal monarch in the name of Almighty God.

63
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.120
[George] The Vandals must spare the lives of the citizens.

64
00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.080
[George] They must inflict no torture or execution.

65
00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:30.240
[George] They must refrain from setting fire to the residential quarters of the city, and above all, they must grant absolute sanctuary to the great Christian basilicas, St.

66
00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.560
[George] Peter's on the Vatican Hill, St.

67
00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:35.360
[George] Paul outside the walls, and the Lateran.

68
00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:44.480
[George] Genseric, recognizing that entering an unburned city without resistance would make systematic looting infinitely easier, gave his royal word.

69
00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:49.120
[George] The gates of Rome were thrown open, and the Vandal occupation began.

70
00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:59.120
[George] For fourteen days, from June 2 to June 16, 455, Rome endured the most systematic, clinical plundering in human history.

71
00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:07.320
[George] The term Vandalism was coined centuries later to describe mindless destruction, but Genseric's sack was anything but mindless.

72
00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:11.000
[George] It was a masterpiece of ruthless logistical efficiency.

73
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:18.120
[George] True to his oath to Pope Leo, Genseric prohibited mass slaughter, torture, and widespread burning.

74
00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:29.320
[George] But every scrap of movable wealth accumulated over a thousand years of Roman imperial conquest was systematically catalogued, carted away, and loaded onto Vandal ships at Ostia.

75
00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:34.120
[George] Hundreds of Vandal wagons rolled through the Roman Forum and the Palatine Hill.

76
00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:41.160
[George] They stripped the gilded bronze tiles from the colossal roof of the temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus on the Capitoline.

77
00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:48.680
[George] They emptied the imperial palaces of priceless Greek statues, gold plate, silks, jewels and furniture.

78
00:09:48.680 --> 00:10:02.920
[George] Inside the imperial treasury, Genseric's men seized the sacred Jewish temple treasures, the golden seven-branched menorah and the table of showbread, which Emperor Titus had brought to Rome after sacking Jerusalem in 70 AD.

79
00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:07.160
[George] Tens of thousands of Roman citizens took refuge within the basilicas of St.

80
00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:08.120
[George] Peter and St.

81
00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:11.560
[George] Paul, where papal clergy kept watch day and night.

82
00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:18.120
[George] True to Genseric's promise to Leo, not a single Vandal soldier breached the doors of the sacred shrines.

83
00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:22.840
[George] However, Genseric did not leave empty-handed when it came to human spoils.

84
00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:34.200
[George] Along with vast treasures, the Vandals took thousands of aristocratic captives to be ransomed or sold into slavery in Africa, including Empress Licinia Eudoxia and her two daughters.

85
00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:45.000
[George] On June 16, 455, the last Vandal galleys weighed anchor and sailed southward toward Carthage, their holds groaning under the weight of Rome's ancient splendor.

86
00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:54.800
[George] When the dust settled, the citizens of Rome emerged from the basilicas to find their cities stripped bare, but remarkably intact and alive.

87
00:10:54.800 --> 00:11:04.160
[George] There had been no wholesale massacre, no burning of monuments, and no annihilation like that which had erased Aquileia or Carthage in centuries past.

88
00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.640
[George] Pope Leo immediately went to work.

89
00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:19.760
[George] The 75-year-old pontiff organized relief kitchens across all fourteen regions of Rome to feed the tens of thousands left destitute by the sack, to redeem Roman captives who had been dragged to North Africa.

90
00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:34.240
[George] Leo ordered the melting down of the remaining silver communion vessels, golden chalices and episcopal plate in the Vatican Treasury, dispatching papal legates to Carthage with chests of bullion to buy back Roman women and children from slavery.

91
00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:50.560
[George] For the remaining six years of his life, Leo oversaw the physical and moral restoration of the city, replacing stolen church altars, establishing monasteries, and instilling in the Roman people a resilient Christian identity that outlasted the collapse of the Roman state.

92
00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:56.880
[George] When Pope Leo died peacefully in November 461, he was buried in the portico of St.

93
00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:04.160
[George] Peter's Basilica, the first bishop of Rome ever to be interred in the sacred resting place of the Apostle Peter.

94
00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:08.240
[Rebecca] Thank you for joining us for this second chapter of Popes of History.

95
00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:15.680
[Rebecca] Pope Leo, I is one of only two popes in 20 centuries to be awarded the title, the Great by Universal Acclamation.

96
00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:29.840
[Rebecca] By halting Attila at the Mincio and extracting mercy from Genseric at the gates of Rome, Leo proved that when military power dissolves and imperial thrones collapse, moral authority and apostolic courage can preserve civilization.

97
00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:43.560
[Rebecca] Tune in next time as we leap forward to the 6th century to follow Pope Gregory the Great, the visionary monk who led Rome through the horrors of the Justinian plague and dispatched missionaries to the ends of the pagan earth.

98
00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:53.320
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

